  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+5,\\,2y,\\,2y(y+5))=2y(y+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=0$ and $y=-5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y} & =\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ 2y(y+5)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}) & =2y(y+5)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y(y+5)}{y+5}+\\frac{2y(y+5)}{2y} & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2y(y+5)}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y\\cancel{(y+5)}}{\\cancel{y+5}}+\\frac{\\cancel{2y}(y+5)}{\\cancel{2y}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2y(y+5)}}{\\cancel{2y(y+5)}} \\\\ 6y+(y+5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+(y+5) & =5 \\\\ 7y+5 & =5 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $y=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions.",
